1
00:00:00,000 --> 00:00:05,500
Agent Spend Firewall: a safety layer between AI agents and Razorpay money.

2
00:00:05,500 --> 00:00:10,500
AI agents can now issue refunds and payouts through Razorpay's MCP tools.

3
00:00:10,500 --> 00:00:15,500
Prompt injection, retry loops or a paise/rupee mix-up can lose real money.

4
00:00:15,500 --> 00:00:20,500
The firewall is a drop-in MCP proxy. The agent connects to it instead of Razorpay.

5
00:00:20,500 --> 00:00:25,500
Reads pass straight through. Money tools need a reason, pass policy-as-code, and get audited.

6
00:00:25,500 --> 00:00:29,200
Live run: the real firewall and default policy, a fake Razorpay and a simulated Telegram approver.

7
00:00:29,200 --> 00:00:35,200
A refund with no reason is rejected before it ever reaches Razorpay.

8
00:00:35,200 --> 00:00:41,200
A legitimate ₹499 refund with a clear reason is allowed and forwarded.

9
00:00:41,200 --> 00:00:47,200
The agent retries the same refund. The firewall blocks the duplicate.

10
00:00:47,200 --> 00:00:54,201
A prompt injection asks for ₹50,000. The hard cap denies it; the reason grants nothing.

11
00:00:54,201 --> 00:01:00,201
Refunding more than what's left on the payment is denied, using a live payment lookup.

12
00:01:00,201 --> 00:01:06,201
₹4,500 is over the ₹2,000 auto-approve limit, so the call is held: NOT executed.

13
00:01:06,201 --> 00:01:11,999
The approver's chat shows the amount, payment, rule and the agent's UNTRUSTED reason.

14
00:01:11,999 --> 00:01:20,201
One tap on Approve sends a signed callback: the firewall executes it exactly once.

15
00:01:20,201 --> 00:01:29,201
Shadow mode: rules only observe. A ₹12,000 refund goes through, logged as "would deny".

16
00:01:29,201 --> 00:01:34,200
Shadow off: back to enforcing. Shadow mode lets you trial a policy on live traffic.

17
00:01:34,200 --> 00:01:39,783
Ops flips the kill switch from the dashboard, behind a confirm step.

18
00:01:39,783 --> 00:01:46,501
The next refund is denied by kill-switch, before any policy runs. Then it is switched off.

19
00:01:46,501 --> 00:01:54,837
Every attempt, approval and outcome lands in a hash-chained, append-only audit log.

20
00:01:54,837 --> 00:02:00,500
Edit one row in the SQLite file and re-verify: the chain is BROKEN at exactly that record.

21
00:02:00,500 --> 00:02:09,000
8 refund attempts, each one explained, bounded, gated and audited.

